commit a56e4fcdcdbbe184ca29f75ce2a5e2c470d56c2a
Author: María Grandury <mariagrandury@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Sep 25 19:05:27 2026 +0200

    ladder: keep each rung at its own batch; put 90M back on the ladder
    
    The 90M and 175M rungs were retrained at batch 84 / 168 (GBS_BY_SIZE,
    2026-09-23) and their diverged batch-504 predecessors are still on disk
    and in older reports. The loader now keeps only the runs at the batch a
    rung uses now (ladder_report.on_grid, the report's own test, repeated at
    the one place every analysis reads from: snr/download/ladder.py), so an
    old run can never share a `family` with its replacement or reach a table.
    
    With the rung sound again, 90M is the ladder's first size everywhere:
    EVAL_SIZES is the whole LADDER (the watcher evaluates the b84 cells on
    its next start), ANALYSIS_SIZES follows, small_sizes gains 90M (a
    decision_acc_size_90M column), check_rules forbids only the sizes above
    the reference, and the two hardcoded size lists that would have raised
    on 90M (paper_ten_checkpoints COLORS, seed_uncertainty's x-limits) read
    the ladder. Rule 10 and the CLAUDE.md / README sentences that said "no
    90M" now say "90M-1.7B at each rung's own batch".
    
    Checked: the guard on a report with fake old-batch 90M/175M cells (dropped)
    next to their b84/b168 replacements and a 350M cell with and without a
    batch token (kept); check_rules 0 findings; unit tests OK. The tables on
    disk still stop at 175M until the pipeline reruns.

